_bundled_

### OS Version

Docker image

### Problem Description

It seems fields which are not indexed are still counted towards `total_fields.limit`. I find this surprising. If the goal of the limit is to limit resources, my understanding is that using `index: false` disables any resources used by the field, effectively making it a no-field. Or is there some other option which has to be also enabled to disable the field fully?

The reason why I found this is because there is no easy way I could find to list all fields created internally based on the mapping. I want to have something like:

```
curl -X PUT "localhost:9200/test?pretty" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d'
{
  "mappings": {
    "dynamic": false,
    "properties": {
      "foobar": {
        "type": "keyword"
      },
      "foobar2": {
        "type": "keyword",
        "index": false,
        "copy_to": "foobar"
      }
    }
  }
}
'
```

And I would expect that only one field is created, with contents from both `foobar` and `foobar2`, but it seems (based on the field limit) that this is not so. Or is the field limit check wrong and in fact only one underlying field gets created? It is not really possible to know.

### Steps to Reproduce

```
curl -X PUT "localhost:9200/test?pretty" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d'
{
  "settings": {
    "index.mapping.total_fields.limit": 1
  },
  "mappings": {
    "dynamic": false,
    "properties": {
      "foobar": {
        "type": "keyword"
      },
      "foobar2": {
        "type": "keyword",
        "index": false
      }
    }
  }
}
'
```

```
{
  "error" : {
    "root_cause" : [
      {
        "type" : "illegal_argument_exception",
        "reason" : "Limit of total fields [1] has been exceeded"
      }
    ],
    "type" : "illegal_argument_exception",
    "reason" : "Limit of total fields [1] has been exceeded"
  },
  "status" : 400
}
```

